  "slug": "rna-targets-7a36",
  "summary": "New preprints pair genome-wide maps, accessibility profiling and binding arrays to nominate RNA regulators and targets in bacteria, protists, yeast and Drosophila.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is systematic discovery of regulatory RNAs and their interaction partners. In bacteria, aureoRNA compiles phylogeny, transcriptome data and target predictions for Staphylococcus aureus sRNAs, revealing new targets such as those of RsaD. A separate study in E. coli profiles CsrA-dependent RNA accessibility to nominate new mRNA and sRNA targets, linking CsrA to polyphosphate levels and Hfq-containing HP-bodies. On the protein side, dsRBP-seq and protein domain arrays screen for direct dsRNA–domain interactions and identify STAU2 dsRBM3 binding to Sem1 dsRNA. Comparative genomics with CMfinder and R-scape adds seven candidate structured ncRNA motifs in protists, likely regulating genes including RuBisCO and phycocyanin. Two studies extend the theme in eukaryotes: multi-colour smFISH resolves distinct, RBP-dependent, cell-cycle-linked localization of the co-regulated yeast mRNAs SRL1 and SUN4, and small ORFs in Drosophila lncRNAs continue translating during heat shock, with some microproteins improving survival.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
